WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:03.070
- [Man] Boeing's popular 787 Dreamliner

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.020
is facing a new manufacturing defect,

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.530
which might sound familiar.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.220
- This is another blow for Boeing.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.380
- [Man] The defect could
take three weeks to address,

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:15.310
which means airlines won't
be receiving Dreamliners

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:19.370
in time for much of the
busy summer travel season.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:20.970
- [Man] Recent issues
with the plane makers

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:24.030
wide body aircraft stretched
back to last summer.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.180
Since then, the defects have
prompted regulatory review,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.540
a halting of deliveries,

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.470
a pile up of more than a hundred planes

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.970
and are now pressuring
Boeing's bottom line.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.630
- This is not a situation
Boeing wants to be in

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:36.840
at this point.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.630
Right now it can't deliver

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:42.490
its most popular wide body aircraft.

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:45.160
It's almost as if the company like Apple

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.520
couldn't deliver laptops.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.850
- [Man] So what are the
issues with the Dreamliner

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.313
and how did Boeing get here?

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.540
First, a bit of background,
the 787 Dreamliner

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.230
made its commercial debut in 2011.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.860
- Costs all sorts of
possibilities for new routes,

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.920
new efficiencies for airlines,

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.690
a better passenger experience

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.160
and then it wounded up on the ground.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.390
- [Man] In early 2013, a fire broke out

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.230
aboard an empty Dreamliner in Boston,

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:14.860
sparked by battery problems
that later surfaced

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.430
on another Dreamliner in Japan.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:22.430
- In two weeks time, we saw
two cases of battery failures

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:29.130
on the 787 and the grounding
of the entire fleet

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:30.567
by the FAA.

00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:33.940
- So the Dreamliner had to
emerge from that experience

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.670
and regain the confidence of airlines

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:37.910
and the traveling public.

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:40.130
- [Man] The 787 Is now one of Boeing's

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.110
most popular aircraft,
more than 60 airlines,

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:44.930
including American and United,

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.570
have added 787s to their fleet.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:50.260
The Dreamliner is unique, as
it was Boeing's first plane

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.540
designed largely by other companies.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.450
Its primary structure is made mostly

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.170
from composite materials for efficiency.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.240
- Because the Dreamliners
structure is new in the world

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.870
of aircraft manufacturing,

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:06.620
Boeing and the FAA are learning
how it's composite materials

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:10.530
and where those materials
join age over time.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.420
- [Man] So fast forward, nearly
a decade of flights later,

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:14.920
to August of last year,

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.770
when Boeing voluntarily
grounded eight Dreamliners

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.820
found with two different
production defects

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.340
in the same location.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.888
- For an aircraft manufacturer
to do that on its own

00:02:23.888 --> 00:02:26.930
is something that you
really don't see that often.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:29.170
- [Man] The defects were
outlined in an August 31st

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.680
internal federal aviation
administration memo,

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.740
which was reviewed and then reported on

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.250
by the Wall Street Journal.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.310
- We first reported about
the Dreamliner issues

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:43.310
in September, when the FAA
launched a high level review

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.830
of Boeing's Dreamliner
manufacturing processes.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.650
At that time, Boeing
and the FAA were trying

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.850
to get their heads around the
problem and they focused on

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.650
one general area of the aircraft.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.300
- [Man] Specifically, the FAA
memo says they were looking

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.250
at nonconforming sections
of the rear fuselage

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.200
or body of the plane that fell short

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.300
of its own engineering standards.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.200
The first defect had to do
with how Boeing used shims

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.730
or the material that fills
the gaps between sections

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.140
of the fuselage.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.080
The second stemmed from variations

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.780
in the smoothness of the fuselage skin.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.800
- While the two types of defects,
independent of each other,

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.820
didn't pose an immediate safety risk,

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.860
together in the same section,

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:28.190
are deemed to potentially introduce a risk

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:33.190
of a structural integrity
problem in extreme circumstances.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.400
- [Man] Then the following day,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:37.300
Boeing flagged a third issue,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.610
this one with sections
of the Dreamliners tail

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.550
that were clamped together
improperly during assembly.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:46.400
- It's not a question of
immediate structural integrity.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.380
It's a question of whether
or not they introduced

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.420
premature aging and if so,

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:53.360
what does that mean for
the Dreamliners in service

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:57.350
and how often they need
to be serviced and fixed?

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.670
- [Man] In a recent internal
video Boeing explained

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.560
how thin these gaps are.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.720
- If you started at the
tail of the airplane,

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.130
just walking to the front,
each of those sections,

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:07.580
when we bring them together,

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.050
is within five thousandths of an inch.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.660
You'll hear people talk of gaps.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:14.960
It's really not what you would
normally think of as a gap.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.930
- [Man] The next month, in October,

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:19.500
Boeing halted delivery of the Dreamliners.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.760
- Halting deliveries is a big deal

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.480
and in fact that means Boeing
can't sell the products.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.350
- [Man] Customers largely
pay Boeing on delivery.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.360
Prior to the halt, many
of Boeing's commercial

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.000
deliveries were Dreamliners,

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.770
as the 737 Max remained grounded
following two fatal crashes

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:35.980
of that aircraft.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:40.300
- Boeing was at that point,
a month away from getting

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.300
the FAA's okay to again,
fly passengers on 737 Max

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.230
and suddenly it couldn't get
its other popular aircraft,

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.600
the Dreamliner, off the lot either.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.490
- [Man] Then with delivery still halted,

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:55.520
The plane maker expanded its inspections

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.330
of the Dreamliner in December,
after it found instances

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:01.130
of the smoothness issue on
new sections of the plane.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:05.060
- At that point, it became
clear that the problem

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:09.690
wasn't going away as quickly
as we might've thought earlier.

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:12.380
- [Man] In mid-March, the FAA
took back Boeing's authority

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:13.850
to inspect and sign off

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:16.130
on several Dreamliners awaiting delivery,

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.880
saying it would conduct
the checks instead.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:23.880
- It had echoes of what happened
after the 737 Max crashes.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.220
This action by the FAA was a sign of

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:32.930
its increasing scrutiny
when it came to overseeing

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:34.950
the manufacturing process
of the Dreamliner.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:35.890
- [Man] But later that month,

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.720
Boeing resumed Dreamliner deliveries,

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:39.410
taking what looked like the first step

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:41.210
toward moving past these issues.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.600
In April, the company's
CEO, David Calhoun,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.840
told investors the 787
delivery pause was an example

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:50.000
of the company trying to solve
its manufacturing issues.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.600
- We've taking actions over
this, really hard actions,

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.410
over the course of this
year to stop things

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:59.010
when we see an issue and get
'em fixed once and for all.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.990
- [Man] Then, in late May,
deliveries were halted again,

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.470
as the FAA requested more
information from Boeing

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.980
about its proposed solution
for catching and addressing

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:10.210
the defects before handing
over the planes to customers.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:14.040
- Boeing was hoping that
it could get FAA approval

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:17.130
for the methodology to inspect,

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:20.170
using a mix of physical
inspections and analysis.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:21.820
- [Man] With those discussions ongoing,

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:24.740
Boeing found the latest
defect around early July.

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:26.540
This time on part of the aircraft

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:28.300
known as the forward pressure bulkhead

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:29.470
at the front of the plane.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:32.190
- So it's going to complicate
the process that was underway

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.220
of going, trying to prove to the FAA

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.430
that its methodology was sound.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.530
- [Man] It's still unclear
when the latest issue

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:41.740
will be resolved, though
Boeing said it could take

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:43.680
at least three weeks to address.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.130
- [Stan] What we're doing on the 787

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:48.190
is a methodical program,

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:50.750
reviewing the airplane from tip to tail,

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:54.150
in a way that's safe and
compliant for our customers

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:55.370
and our regulators.

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:58.150
- [Man] Meanwhile, finished
Dreamliners are piling up.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:00.460
More than a hundred planes await delivery.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:02.350
Boeing has slowed production on the plane

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:04.730
and said it now expects
to deliver less than half

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:07.480
of its 787 inventory this year.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:10.080
- [Man] The slowdown is likely to ripple

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.200
into Boeing's vast supply network

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.120
and for some weeks and months,

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.050
it's going to mean that
Boeing's airline customers

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:24.170
have to wait longer to
get their new Dreamliners.

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:26.610
- [Man] It's unclear whether
these manufacturing issues

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.500
will matter in the long run.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.700
- Whether or not there
is any long-term impacts,

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:36.660
good or bad, to Boeing's
reputation remains to be seen.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:40.880
For now, the Dreamliner is
still a very popular aircraft

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:43.091
and has an excellent safety record.

00:07:43.091 --> 00:07:45.508
(calm music)

